{"name":"Full Moon","hex":"#f4f3e0","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"full moon","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The phase of the moon when it is in opposition to the sun and its full disc is therefore visible.","The moon when it is in opposition to the sun."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/full_moon"}],"entries":[{"title":"Full moon","blocks":[{"text":"The full moon is the lunar phase when the Moon appears fully illuminated from Earth's perspective. This occurs when Earth is located between the Sun and the Moon. This means that the lunar hemisphere facing Earth—the near side—is completely sunlit and appears as an approximately circular disk. The full moon occurs roughly once a month."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Pournami.jpg/330px-Pournami.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Full_moon","wikidata":"Q104641"},{"title":"Full Moon (Brandy album)","blocks":[{"text":"Full Moon is the third studio album by American singer Brandy. It was released on February 20, 2002, by Atlantic Records. The album was recorded primarily during the summer and fall of 2001 at the Hit Factory Criteria in Miami, amid a three-year musical hiatus following the success of her multi-platinum previous studio album Never Say Never (1998) and the finale of her successful television sitcom Moesha in May 2001. As with Never Say Never, Brandy collaborated with producer Rodney Jerkins and his Darkchild production and songwriting team on the majority of the album's composition, while Mike City, Warryn Campbell, and Keith Crouch contributed additional production."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/6/68/Brandy-Full_Moon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Full_Moon_(Brandy_album)","wikidata":"Q3107280"},{"title":"Moon","blocks":[{"text":"The Moon is the only natural satellite of Earth. It orbits Earth in an elliptical path, at a distance that varies over the year, averaging around 378,000 kilometers (235,000 mi). It completes an orbit in relation to Earth and the Sun (synodically) every 29.5 days. The Moon and Earth are bound by gravitational attraction, which is stronger on the sides facing each other. The resulting tidal forces are the main driver of Earth's tides, and have pulled the Moon to always face Earth with the same near side. This tidal locking effectively synchronizes the Moon's rotation period to its orbital period."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/FullMoon2010.jpg/330px-FullMoon2010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moon","wikidata":"Q405"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/FullMoon2020.jpg/500px-FullMoon2020.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/FullMoon2020.jpg/1280px-FullMoon2020.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/FullMoon2020.jpg/500px-FullMoon2020.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A full moon was taken on August 3, 2020","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFullMoon2020.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Full_moon%2C_March_13%2C_2025._Taken_with_a_Nikon_Z6II_camera_and_Sigma_150-600mm_f_5-6.3_DG_OS_HSM_lens.jpg/500px-Full_moon%2C_March_13%2C_2025._Taken_with_a_Nikon_Z6II_camera_and_Sigma_150-600mm_f_5-6.3_DG_OS_HSM_lens.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Full_moon%2C_March_13%2C_2025._Taken_with_a_Nikon_Z6II_camera_and_Sigma_150-600mm_f_5-6.3_DG_OS_HSM_lens.jpg/500px-Full_moon%2C_March_13%2C_2025._Taken_with_a_Nikon_Z6II_camera_and_Sigma_150-600mm_f_5-6.3_DG_OS_HSM_lens.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Full moon, 13 March 2025. Taken with a Nikon Z6II camera and Sigma 150-600mm f 5-6.3 DG OS HSM lens","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFull_moon%2C_March_13%2C_2025._Taken_with_a_Nikon_Z6II_camera_and_Sigma_150-600mm_f_5-6.3_DG_OS_HSM_lens.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Moon_and_red_blue_haze.jpg/500px-Moon_and_red_blue_haze.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Moon_and_red_blue_haze.jpg/1280px-Moon_and_red_blue_haze.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Moon_and_red_blue_haze.jpg/500px-Moon_and_red_blue_haze.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A full moon rising, seen through the Belt of Venus","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMoon_and_red_blue_haze.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Harvest_moon.jpg/500px-Harvest_moon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Harvest_moon.jpg/500px-Harvest_moon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A harvest moon. Its orange color is due to greater Rayleigh scattering as the Moon appears close above the horizon, rather than being unique to harvest moons.[14]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHarvest_moon.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Christmas_full_moons_1977-2091.gif/330px-Christmas_full_moons_1977-2091.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Christmas_full_moons_1977-2091.gif/330px-Christmas_full_moons_1977-2091.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The December 2015 full moon coincided with Christmas.[44] This last occurred in 1977 (for the American time zones). A small horizontal libration is visible comparing their appearances. By the 19-year metonic cycle the full moon will repeat on Christmas Day in 2034, 2053, 2072, and 2091.[45]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChristmas_full_moons_1977-2091.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Rodney_Darkchild_Jerkins_%2811149414586%29.jpg/500px-Rodney_Darkchild_Jerkins_%2811149414586%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Rodney_Darkchild_Jerkins_%2811149414586%29.jpg/500px-Rodney_Darkchild_Jerkins_%2811149414586%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Brandy reteamed with Rodney Jerkins (pictured) and his team to work the majority of Full Moon.[8]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARodney_Darkchild_Jerkins_(11149414586).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/LJ-MakedaSandford-BkShoot.jpg/500px-LJ-MakedaSandford-BkShoot.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/LJ-MakedaSandford-BkShoot.jpg/1280px-LJ-MakedaSandford-BkShoot.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/LJ-MakedaSandford-BkShoot.jpg/500px-LJ-MakedaSandford-BkShoot.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Luke James described the album as the \"Bible\" of contemporary R&B from the 2000s, calling it \"the diagram on how to make vocals.\"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALJ-MakedaSandford-BkShoot.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Big_Splash_Theia.gif/500px-Big_Splash_Theia.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Big_Splash_Theia.gif/500px-Big_Splash_Theia.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"This not-to-scale animation shows (in an Earth and Sun stationary rotating frame of reference) the formation of the Moon through the collision of the hypothetical Mars-sized planet named Theia with Earth. After orbiting for tens of millions of years at the L4 Lagrange point as an Earth-trojan, Theia's orbit comes in \"loops\" finally too close to Earth.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABig_Splash_Theia.gif"}],"sounds":{"full moon":[{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/88/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vininn126-full_moon.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/8/88/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vininn126-full_moon.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vininn126-full_moon.wav.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/8/88/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vininn126-full_moon.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vininn126-full_moon.wav.ogg"}]},"pictures":[{"title":"Blue-Beige Gradient","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Blue-Beige_Gradient.jpg/500px-Blue-Beige_Gradient.jpg","ratio":1.6,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blue-Beige_Gradient.jpg","share":0.335625},{"title":"Full moon partially obscured by atmosphere","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Full_moon_partially_obscured_by_atmosphere.jpg/500px-Full_moon_partially_obscured_by_atmosphere.jpg",